#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161754 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/id #uuid "2ac70ed6-4f03-59ae-9a3b-b8a93386507d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8021" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5c5e52c78f925dc4e01b1098b507865407f9848cc1db6e8a946ad81840e8e4ee.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X \\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\n1. $f(2) = 4$\n2. $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X$, we have $f(x) \\neq y$\n3. The function $f:X \\to Y$ is **into**" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X$:\n\n> $f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X$, we have $f(x) \\neq y$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, there is an arrow from $2 \\in X$ to $4\\in Y$. So, $f(2)=4$.\n- Statement II is true. According to the diagram, $1 \\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $1 \\in Y$. As a result, there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=1$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x) \\neq y$ for any $x$ in $X$.\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161755 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ad9700-5aaa-5b00-8d2f-acce6ed59c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "346ff48a-b54f-56cc-b5b2-4437c7d3d94d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8022" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ which is given by $f(x)=(x-1)^2-2$?\n\n1. The range of $f$ is $[-2, \\infty)$\n2. $\\forall\\, y \\in [-2, \\infty)$, $\\exists\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x)=y$\n3. $f$ maps $\\mathbb{R}$ **into** $\\mathbb{R}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/35/35949b4a4809821871a9edf9466fa00862778094d754674014407525b5fc2fed.png)\n\nRecall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X$:\n\n> $f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X$, we have $f(x) \\neq y$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the lowest $y$-value is $-2$, and it occurs at the point $(1,-2)$. The graph will cover all $y$-values higher than $y=-2$, including $y=-2$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $[-2, \\infty)$.\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $[-2, \\infty)$, then for all $y$ contained within $[-2, \\infty)$, there exists some $x$ contained within $\\mathbb{R}$ such that $f(x) = y$.\n- Statement III is true. As we can see from the range, there is no real number $x$ such that $f(x)=-3 \\in \\mathbb{R}$. So, $f$ maps $\\mathbb{R}$ *into* $\\mathbb{R}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161756 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval"}] :question/id #uuid "d3e8ba8d-5a83-5683-9e19-33f021459ceb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8023" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\n1. $f: [-2, \\infty) \\to \\mathbb{R}$ defined by $f(x) =\\sqrt{x+2}$\n2. $g: \\mathbb{R} \\to [0, \\infty)$ defined by $g(x) = \\mid x-2 \\mid +3$\n3. $h:(0,\\infty) \\to\\Bbb R$ defined by $h(x) = \\ln(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f: X \\rightarrow Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X$:\n\n> $f: X \\to Y$ is an into function if $\\exists\\, y \\in Y$ such that $\\forall\\, x \\in X$, we have $f(x) \\neq y$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is an into function. Let's take $-1 \\in\\Bbb R$. There is no number $x \\in[-2, \\infty)$ such that $f(x) =\\sqrt{x+2}=-1$. In fact, $f(x) =\\sqrt{x+2} \\geq 0$ for all $x \\in[-2, \\infty)$.\n- II is an into function. Let's take $1 \\in [0, \\infty)$. There is no number $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $g(x)= \\mid x-2 \\mid +3=1$. In fact, $\\mid x-2 \\mid +3 \\geq 3$ for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n- III is a *not* an into function. Given any $y \\in \\Bbb R$, we can choose $x =e^{y} \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then $h(x) = h \\left(e^{y}\\right) =\\ln(e^{y}) = y$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90e92f9e-b28b-5fd5-9a0d-26508bc7911a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158613} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ca7bed-282b-5eb9-bbe1-b6c17a1d0d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158614} {:answer/id #uuid "ce213c96-39b7-5ff1-9673-abc975004efa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158615} {:answer/id #uuid "195683e8-fbe6-52f0-bf15-a80cb39f2460" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 1759218604549